==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈbɹəʊkə/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|/ˈbɹoʊkɚ/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|əʊkə(ɹ)|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{suffix|broke|er|lang=en}}.

====Adjective====
{{head|en|adjective comparative form}}

# {{en-comparative of|broke}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|broker}}, {{m|enm|brokour}}, {{m|enm|brocour}}, from {{der|en|xno|brocour||small trader}} (compare also {{m|fro|abroker||to act as a broker}}), from {{der|en|odt|*brokere||one who determines the usages of trade, manager}}, from {{m|odt|broke}}, {{m|odt|bruyck}}, {{m|odt|breuck||use, usage, trade}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*brūkiz||use, custom}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰruHg-||to use, enjoy}}, equivalent to {{suffix|brook|er|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{cog|gml|brukere||a broker}}, {{cog|da|bruger||a broker, user, handler}}, {{cog|sv|bruk||use, custom, trade, business}}, {{cog|ang|broc||use, profit, advantage, foredeal}}. Compare also {{cog|fr|brocanter||to deal in second-hand goods}} from the same Germanic source. More at {{m|en|brook}}.

====Noun====
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# A [[mediator]] between a [[buyer]] and [[seller]].
# A [[stockbroker]].
# A [[mediator]] in general, one who liaises between two or more parties to attempt to achieve an outcome of some kind.
#* '''2014''', Spencer C. Tucker, ''World War I: The Definitive Encyclopedia and Document Collection'', {{ISBN|1851099654}}, [https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=DBwTBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA243&dq=%22broker+of+peace%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwidudT68dnLAhXIGD4KHYJiCCwQ6AEIRzAF#v=onepage&q=%22broker%20of%20peace%22&f=false page 244]
#*: The peace plan was representative of Benedict's inability to appear as a neutral '''broker''' of peace
# {{lb|en|computing}} An agent involved in the exchange of messages or transactions.

